@*
    This file is part of the ASP.NET Core project template.
    It is used to display error information when an unhandled exception occurs.
    You can customize this view to provide a better user experience for your application.

     The @model directive specifies the type of the model that will be used in this view. In this case, it is an instance of the ErrorViewModel class.
    The ErrorViewModel class typically contains properties such as RequestId and ShowRequestId, which are used to display error information to the user.
*@
@model ErrorViewModel
@*
    The ViewData dictionary is used to pass data from the controller to the view. In this case, we are setting the "Title" key in the ViewData dictionary to "Error".
    This value can be accessed in the layout or other parts of the view to display the title of the page.
*@
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Error";
}
@*
    The layout is set to the _AccountLayout.cshtml file, which provides a consistent look and feel for account-related pages.
    You can customize the layout or create a new one if needed.
*@
<h1 class="text-danger">Error.</h1>
<h2 class="text-danger">An error occurred while processing your request.</h2>
@*
    The following section displays the Request ID if the ShowRequestId property of the model is true.
    This can be useful for troubleshooting and identifying specific requests that caused errors.
*@
@if (Model.ShowRequestId)
{
    <p>
        <strong>Request ID:</strong> <code>@Model.RequestId</code>
    </p>
}
@*
    The following section provides information about the development mode and how to enable it for local debugging.
    It is important to note that the development environment should not be enabled for deployed applications, as it may expose sensitive information.
*@
<h3>Development Mode</h3>
<p>
    Swapping to <strong>Development</strong> environment will display more detailed information about the error that occurred.
</p>
@*
    The following section provides a warning about enabling the development environment in deployed applications.
    It emphasizes the importance of keeping sensitive information secure and provides instructions for enabling the development environment for local debugging.
*@
<p>
    <strong>The Development environment shouldn't be enabled for deployed applications.</strong>
    It can result in displaying sensitive information from exceptions to end users.
    For local debugging, enable the <strong>Development</strong> environment by setting the <strong>ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT</strong> environment variable to <strong>Development</strong>
    and restarting the app.
</p>
